WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.810
Andrew Huberman: Welcome to the Huberman
Lab podcast, where we discuss science and

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.730
science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.707
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.960
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor
of neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.550
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.650
Today we're discussing water.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.940
Now, to some of you, water might seem
like a boring topic, but I assure you

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.950
that water is anything but a boring topic.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.480
In fact, water as a substance is
incredibly interesting for a variety

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.310
of reasons that I'll explain today.

00:00:29.315 --> 00:00:32.670
In fact, we are going to discuss the
physics and chemistry of water, and

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.390
I promise to make it accessible to
anyone and everyone regardless of

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.240
whether or not you have a physics
or chemistry background, and I will

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.340
discuss how your body needs and
utilizes water depending on what type

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.390
of water you drink, the temperature of
that water, when you drink the water,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.850
and indeed how you drink that water.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.940
Now, water is actually a
pretty controversial topic.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.020
In fact, in preparing for this episode,
which took me several months, in

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.150
fact, I ran into highly contradictory
information and had to go on some real

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.860
deep dives in order to ferret out the
best and most accurate knowledge for you.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.310
I also found that there are generally
two camps of people out there

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.940
in terms of how they think about
water and the consumption of water.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.380
One camp generally speaking, is of the
mind that tap water is completely safe.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.220
Perhaps it needs a little bit of
filtering, but that in most areas

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.130
of the world, if it runs out of the
tap, and unless there's a warning

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.620
sign directly above the faucet,
that you can drink the tap water.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.010
The other camp seems to be the camp
that does not trust anything that comes

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:40.140
out of the tap and is excited by and
in fact, relies on things like reverse

00:01:40.145 --> 00:01:45.480
osmosis, deuterium depleted, hydrogen
rich, or other forms of water that

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.230
sometimes can be very expensive or at
least involve some substantial steps

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:55.050
in order to clean, filter, alter the
chemistry of, or in some other way, adjust

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.790
before they are willing to consume it.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:02.310
So today what we're going to try and do is
to address all the stances around water.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.570
For instance, we will discuss whether
or not tap water is indeed safe, and I

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.990
will give you some tools that will allow
you to address whether or not the water

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.050
coming out of your tap is safe, as well
as some tools that will allow you to

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.700
address how to clean that water if indeed
it does need filtering and cleaning.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.220
In particular for things like fluorides
and endocrine disruptors, which it turns

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.710
out, are quite prominent in a lot, not
all, but a lot of tap water sources.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.339
I will also talk about the more
"esoteric forms of water" that

00:02:31.339 --> 00:02:32.470
I mentioned a few minutes ago.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.610
So I will go systematically through
the list of distilled reverse osmosis,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.320
spring water, deuterium depleted
water, high pH water, and for those

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.360
of you that are already screaming out
as you hear this, oh no, he's going

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.890
to tell us that pH water can alter
the pH of our body in helpful ways.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.420
I'm not going to tell you that, but I will
tell you that the alkalinity or acidity of

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.500
the water that is the pH of the water that
you drink has a profound impact on your

00:02:56.505 --> 00:03:02.180
ability to absorb and utilize that water
and the impact that those water molecules

00:03:02.185 --> 00:03:04.360
have on specific biological systems.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.530
So it turns out pH is very important,
but not for the reasons that you've

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.150
probably heard about previously.

00:03:10.495 --> 00:03:13.855
I'll talk about how the temperature of
water that you drink does indeed turn

00:03:13.855 --> 00:03:17.905
out to be important for the rate of
absorption of that water and its impact

00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:20.785
on the cells, tissues, and organs of
your body, and thereby your health.

00:03:20.935 --> 00:03:24.805
And I will talk about various zero
cost and low cost tools that you

00:03:24.805 --> 00:03:28.345
can use in order to get the most
out of the water that you drink.

00:03:28.615 --> 00:03:32.605
And finally, I will talk about when
and how to hydrate your body best.

00:03:32.695 --> 00:03:35.995
Before we dive into today's topic,
I wanna share with you some very

00:03:35.995 --> 00:03:39.055
interesting results that were just
published on the use of deliberate

00:03:39.055 --> 00:03:41.725
cold exposure to benefit fat loss.

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:45.475
Now, deliberate cold exposure is a topic
I've covered before on this podcast.

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:48.445
We have an entire episode about that, that
I've linked in the show note captions.

00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:52.584
Deliberate cold exposure can be done
by way of cold showers or immersion

00:03:52.584 --> 00:03:54.385
in cold or ice water up to the neck.

00:03:54.445 --> 00:03:56.755
That's typically the ways that it's done.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.460
It has been shown to reduce inflammation,
to increase metabolism, and I think some

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.610
of the most exciting results that have
been published are the results certainly

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.570
in humans showing that deliberate cold
exposure can increase the release of

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.430
so-called catecholamines, which are
dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.510
And those increases in those three
molecules are quite long lasting

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:23.250
and lead to substantial increases in
mood and focus throughout the day.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.450
Now, many people out there hear about
deliberate cold exposure and cringe.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.080
Other people hear about it and cringe
because they've heard that deliberate cold

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.790
exposure, especially by way of immersion
in water, can block the adaptation

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.380
to strength or hypertrophy training.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.300
What I mean by that is yes, indeed there
are data showing that if one gets into

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:48.105
very cold water up to the neck in the six
hours, anytime that is in the six hours

00:04:48.105 --> 00:04:52.335
after strength or hypertrophy training,
that some of the strength and hypertrophy

00:04:52.705 --> 00:04:56.325
increases that one would observe, are
blocked by that deliberate cold exposure.

00:04:56.325 --> 00:04:59.235
However, after six hours does
not seem to be a problem.

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:02.655
So it can be done on other days besides
the strength and hypertrophy training.

00:05:02.775 --> 00:05:04.755
It can be done before strength
and hypertrophy training.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.435
It can be done after endurance work.

00:05:06.915 --> 00:05:12.285
And I should mention that it does not
appear that cold showers disrupt the

00:05:12.285 --> 00:05:15.615
adaptations to strength and hypertrophy
training, even if they're done immediately

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:17.115
after strength or hypertrophy training.

00:05:17.265 --> 00:05:21.615
Okay, with that said, many people do enjoy
the effects of deliberate cold exposure,

00:05:21.615 --> 00:05:25.995
in particular for those increases in mood
and alertness that are the consequence

00:05:25.995 --> 00:05:29.595
of those increases in the catecholamine,
dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine.

00:05:30.105 --> 00:05:32.355
And again, those increases
are very long lasting.

00:05:32.355 --> 00:05:34.575
So it's not just during
the exposure to cold.

00:05:34.905 --> 00:05:38.535
It is for several hours up to
four, maybe even five or six hours,

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:43.215
depending on how cold and how long the
deliberate cold exposure happens to be.

00:05:43.425 --> 00:05:46.725
Again, there's a lot to say and
explore about deliberate cold exposure.

00:05:46.965 --> 00:05:49.635
So again, I'll just refer you to the
episode on deliberate cold exposure.

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:53.145
If you want to explore the mechanisms
and the positive health outcomes,

00:05:53.475 --> 00:05:56.955
some of the controversies within
the data, etc., within that episode.

00:05:57.225 --> 00:05:59.685
Meanwhile, I definitely want to
share with you the results of this

00:05:59.685 --> 00:06:01.755
recent study that just came out.

00:06:01.755 --> 00:06:05.775
The title of this study is "Impact
of Cold Exposure on Life Satisfaction

00:06:05.835 --> 00:06:08.145
and Physical Composition of Soldiers".

00:06:08.475 --> 00:06:11.835
The reason this study is very interesting
is that it's one of the few studies

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:16.664
that used, or I should say, explored
both deliberate cold exposure by

00:06:16.664 --> 00:06:21.075
immersion in cold water, as well as
deliberate cold exposure by way of cold

00:06:21.075 --> 00:06:23.835
showers as it relates to weight loss.

00:06:24.224 --> 00:06:27.224
Now, there's already data out there on
the effects of deliberate cold exposure

00:06:27.224 --> 00:06:30.945
and metabolism, and here I'm mainly
referring to the beautiful work of Dr.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.354
Susanna Søberg and colleagues in
Scandinavia that showed that people that

00:06:35.354 --> 00:06:39.585
do 11 minutes total of deliberate cold
exposure by immersion and cold water

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.424
up to the neck per week, so 11 minutes
per week total, spread out across some

00:06:43.424 --> 00:06:48.224
different sessions by way of getting into
water that's uncomfortably cold up to the

00:06:48.224 --> 00:06:51.525
neck, and then getting out and then doing
that several times per week to hit that

00:06:51.525 --> 00:06:55.484
11 minutes or more threshold, and, this
is very important, we'll come up in a

00:06:55.484 --> 00:07:01.215
moment in the context of this new study,
and warming up not by getting into a warm

00:07:01.219 --> 00:07:06.284
shower, which is frankly what I do after
my cold showers or getting into the ice

00:07:06.284 --> 00:07:11.804
bath or cold water immersion, but rather
forcing their body to warm up naturally

00:07:12.344 --> 00:07:15.164
by using its own metabolic abilities.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.400
In those studies, they observed
substantial increases in brown fat stores,

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.040
which are fat stores that you really want
around the heart, and clavicles increases

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:29.190
in metabolism that were quite dramatic
in my opinion, and that could be very

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.460
beneficial for allowing people to feel
more comfortable at cold temperatures when

00:07:32.465 --> 00:07:34.980
they're not in cold water and on and on.

00:07:34.985 --> 00:07:36.720
So lots of benefits shown in that study.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.560
In this study, what I thought was
particularly interesting is, again,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:45.120
they explored both immersion in
cold water and cold showers, and the

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.080
duration of cold exposure that they
found led to substantial fat loss,

00:07:49.085 --> 00:07:53.850
especially around the abdomen, was
very brief, deliberate cold exposure.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.160
Let me give you a few
details about this study.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:02.130
The study involved 49 subjects that
include both males and females.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.240
This is also really important.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.510
The beautiful work of Susanna
Søberg and colleagues, as far

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:09.000
as I know, only looked at males.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.620
This study looked at males and females.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:15.120
They were 19 to 30 years old, and there
basically were two groups, people who

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.310
either were assigned to get deliberate
cold exposure, or they were not

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:20.820
assigned to deliberate cold exposure.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:26.580
The form of deliberate cold exposure
involved one session per week of cold

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.130
immersion in cold water up to the neck.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.210
And to just give you a sense of
how cold it was, it was 3 degrees

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:37.530
Celsius, which translates to
about 37.5 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.799
That's pretty darn cold, but
it was only for two minutes.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:48.390
Okay, so one session at 3 degrees Celsius,
otherwise known as 37.4 degrees Fahrenheit

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.230
for two minutes every week, once a week.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.510
In addition, the same subjects did
five cold showers per week, or a

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.550
minimum of five cold showers per week.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:03.780
And those cold showers were
slightly warmer than the

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.220
immersion in cold water condition.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.510
So they were 10 degrees Celsius
approximately, or 50 degrees

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:10.770
Fahrenheit, still pretty cold.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.280
And the duration of that cold
water exposure in the shower

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.950
was just for 30 seconds.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.640
Okay, so this is interesting to
me because many people don't have

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.760
access to cold water immersion.

00:09:23.765 --> 00:09:25.920
They might not have an ice bath
or any place they can do that, but

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.070
they, most people do have access
to a cold shower of some sort.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.420
Plus, I think most people could
do probably one ice bath per

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.060
week or find a place where they
could get into cold water safely.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:40.140
Now, I should point out that some
people will not do well going

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.780
into 37.5 degree Fahrenheit, aka 3
degrees Celsius water, having never

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:46.980
done anything like this before.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.740
So if you're going to try and employ
these sorts of protocols that were used

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.830
in the study, I do recommend that you
ease into it over the course of a week

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:57.390
or so and become somewhat adapted to
the, the shock of cold water exposure.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.780
So maybe start at, you know, 50
degrees Fahrenheit, kind of ease

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.420
your way back in terms of the
cold water immersion, especially.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.670
Now, another critical feature of
this study is, as with the beautiful

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:14.730
work by Susanna Søberg, the subjects
were told to warm up naturally

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.010
after the deliberate cold exposure.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.670
So they basically hung out outside of
the cold water immersion or outside of

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:25.800
the cold shower for 10 minutes after
they were exposed to the cold in their

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.700
bathing suit, or I'm, I'm assuming they
were wearing something, but the point is

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:35.190
that you are not going from deliberate
cold exposure directly into a hot shower

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.780
or a sauna or something of that sort.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.510
So again, their bodies were forced to heat
up again naturally after the deliberate

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.770
cold exposure, but after the 10 minute
period, they were able to do whatever they

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.670
wanted, essentially, reclothe, take a warm
shower, and so on and go about their day.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:54.220
Now the results of this deliberate
cold exposure protocol, again, 2

00:10:54.245 --> 00:10:59.100
minutes in cold immersion at 3 degrees
Celsius, 37.5 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:05.010
plus 5 cold showers per week of 2
minutes long, a little bit warmer, 10

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.050
degrees Celsius, 50 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.840
Now, the deliberate cold exposure
used in this study caused

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:13.140
many different statistically
significant positive changes.

00:11:14.235 --> 00:11:17.505
They had a very extensive questionnaire
that related to mood, everything

00:11:17.505 --> 00:11:21.585
from levels of anxiety to sexual
satisfaction, and on and on.

00:11:21.585 --> 00:11:25.335
In fact, they saw a statistically
significant improvement in sexual

00:11:25.335 --> 00:11:29.055
satisfaction in the subjects that were
exposed to deliberate cold exposure.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:33.075
Not in the control group, although they
didn't look at this, chances are those

00:11:33.075 --> 00:11:36.555
improvements in sexual satisfaction
were the downstream consequence of

00:11:36.555 --> 00:11:41.175
the known increases in testosterone
and free testosterone that occur

00:11:41.175 --> 00:11:44.925
in both men and women who do the
sorts of deliberate cold exposure.

00:11:44.985 --> 00:11:49.425
Again, testosterone being an important
hormone for libido in both men and women.

00:11:50.685 --> 00:11:55.605
They also saw improvements in regulation
of anxiety, which I think is very

00:11:55.605 --> 00:11:59.445
interesting given that the deliberate
cold exposure often causes people anxiety.

00:11:59.505 --> 00:12:04.065
But here and in other studies we've
seen it can lead to an better ability

00:12:04.065 --> 00:12:07.905
to buffer against anxiety in the
normal happenings of everyday life.

00:12:09.705 --> 00:12:12.885
Perhaps the most interesting and
significant results that they found

00:12:12.885 --> 00:12:17.295
in the study however, were that in
particular in men, there was a reduction

00:12:17.295 --> 00:12:21.465
in waste circumference following 8
weeks of this deliberate cold exposure,

00:12:21.885 --> 00:12:28.215
as well as a 5.5% on average, 5.5%
reduction in abdominal fat, that

00:12:28.215 --> 00:12:31.605
was quite statistically significant
when compared to the other groups.

00:12:31.605 --> 00:12:37.305
Now, why there was no observed reduction
in abdominal fat or waist circumference

00:12:37.365 --> 00:12:39.314
in the female subjects isn't clear.

00:12:39.465 --> 00:12:43.035
Could have to do with just the
way that body fat is stored and

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.345
metabolized in females versus males.

00:12:45.645 --> 00:12:47.895
That is going to be a topic
for future exploration.

00:12:48.165 --> 00:12:51.675
So I do think the study is very
interesting because when you look at

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:55.335
the landscape of science and discussion
around deliberate cold exposure, I

00:12:55.335 --> 00:12:58.125
think there's a general consensus
now that deliberate cold exposure

00:12:58.245 --> 00:13:02.595
can change one's sense of mood and
wellbeing through this increases in

00:13:02.595 --> 00:13:04.064
catecholamines that I mentioned earlier.

00:13:04.485 --> 00:13:09.255
But the impact on metabolism itself has
been somewhat controversial because the

00:13:09.255 --> 00:13:13.425
overall changes in metabolism that are
observed while statistically significant

00:13:13.425 --> 00:13:18.074
in many studies, have not ever really been
shown to translate into weight loss or

00:13:18.074 --> 00:13:20.145
body fat loss in any kind of specific way.

00:13:20.295 --> 00:13:25.395
And of course, a great advantage of this
study is that by exploring soldiers,

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:30.420
they were able to really hold constant a
number of other features like the amount

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:34.980
of daily activity that those soldiers
are exposed to, their diet, their living

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:36.510
conditions, and so on and so forth.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.300
So at least insofar as human
studies are done, it's a, it's

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.470
a very well controlled study.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.780
We'll provide a link to the
study in the show note captions.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:46.439
And for those of you that are thinking
about employing the protocol that's

00:13:46.439 --> 00:13:50.460
used in this particular paper, or
combining it with existing deliberate

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.430
cold exposure protocols, to me it
seems pretty straightforward in

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:54.930
of pretty minimal time investment.

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:58.980
Just 2 minutes of deliberate cold
exposure by way of water immersion

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:04.230
up to the neck, and 5 times a week
of 30 seconds each of deliberate

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.030
cold exposure by way of cold shower.

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:07.710
And just a quick mention
about cold showers.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:11.280
If you're going to use cold showers
to do deliberate cold exposure,

00:14:11.939 --> 00:14:15.685
you're going to want to stand
under the shower itself, right?

00:14:15.685 --> 00:14:19.515
And essentially have it hit your head,
the back of your neck and your upper

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.785
back, which is where most of your
brown fat stores are concentrated.

00:14:22.785 --> 00:14:26.025
And it turns out that cold exposure to
those regions of the body in particular,

00:14:26.265 --> 00:14:29.955
are going to trigger the adaptation of
increased brown fat stores, which involves

00:14:29.955 --> 00:14:31.395
increases in mitochondria in those fat.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.675
Again, this is not the
blubbery fat beneath the skin.

00:14:33.675 --> 00:14:37.594
This is the fat that acts as kind of
an oil in the furnace that is your

