problem, but a solution for how water

00:43:08.970 --> 00:43:10.620
can get across that lipid barrier.

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:11.870
Why?

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:16.710
It is the fact that water can change its
confirmation and lipids can change their

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:21.480
confirmation just enough so that the
bonds between water and the bonds between

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:27.660
those hydrophobic lipids can interact,
allowing the water molecule to basically

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:32.040
pass through the lipid because it can
bond very weakly or in some cases, not at

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:35.850
all, but very weakly to those lipids and
then be pushed through to the other side.

00:43:36.089 --> 00:43:36.810
Really incredible.

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:40.470
If you think about it, if there was too
much of a hydrophobic relationship between

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:44.954
the lipid and the water, the water would
come up to the surface of that fatty

00:43:44.954 --> 00:43:48.140
outside  of our cells, and then would
be repelled away from it, or it would

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.500
just stay there right at the surface.

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:52.290
And that would be no good because
we actually need that water to

00:43:52.379 --> 00:43:55.830
diffuse across the cell membranes, or
actually it's a double cell membrane,

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:57.390
as I mentioned before, two layers.

00:43:57.899 --> 00:44:02.370
So water and lipids of cells can
interact with just enough affinity

00:44:02.819 --> 00:44:06.839
that the water molecule can diffuse
across those cell membrane barriers.

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:12.370
But, and this is an important but, the
diffusion of water molecules across

00:44:12.370 --> 00:44:18.440
those lipid barriers on the outsides of
cells is a fairly slow process compared

00:44:18.445 --> 00:44:22.710
to the other way that water accesses
cells and this other way that water

00:44:22.710 --> 00:44:26.610
accesses cells is really something that
was just discovered about 10 years ago.

00:44:26.615 --> 00:44:29.790
So this is a fairly recent discovery,
but turns out to be a fundamental

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:33.480
discovery, which is the presence
of water called aquaporin channels.

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:39.720
Aquaporin channels are basically portals
through the membrane that allow water

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:45.690
molecules to move very quickly across cell
membranes at a rate of about 1 million

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:49.470
H₂O, 1 million water molecules per second.

00:44:49.650 --> 00:44:54.180
And the way that water molecules move
across the cell membrane through those

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:56.340
aquaporin channels is very interesting.

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.470
The inside of those channels,
and the way you think of these

00:44:58.475 --> 00:45:02.279
is they're literally tubes stuck
through the membranes of cells.

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:07.770
The insides of those channels are
very hydrophobic, allowing those

00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:11.490
water molecules to just jot really
quickly and almost as if in your

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:14.100
mind, you can just imagine as if it
was sort of lubricated for the water.

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:17.580
Although it's not really lubricated,
the the water molecules can move through

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:21.150
in single file a million per second.

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:26.940
Now, why would you need two ways for
water to get across cell membranes?

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:32.565
One fairly slow through  basic diffusion,
and again, diffusion folks is the movement

00:45:32.565 --> 00:45:36.315
of things from a gradient of higher
concentration to lower concentration,

00:45:36.615 --> 00:45:39.825
which you just think about this as
things tend to run downhill from higher

00:45:39.825 --> 00:45:41.595
concentration to lower concentration.

00:45:41.685 --> 00:45:44.755
They try and create
equilibrium  across space.

00:45:45.105 --> 00:45:48.279
So, you know, if you had a bunch
of marbles on one side  of a box,

00:45:48.295 --> 00:45:52.065
they're just imagine that these
were water molecules because of the

00:45:52.065 --> 00:45:56.055
charges between those hydrogens and
oxygens, there's a tendency for those

00:45:56.625 --> 00:46:00.525
marbles to spread out and essentially
take on a fairly even confirmation.

00:46:00.795 --> 00:46:04.035
That's basically just
diffusion across a space.

00:46:04.635 --> 00:46:07.305
Water molecules will also move
from higher concentration to lower

00:46:07.305 --> 00:46:11.205
concentration cross cell membranes,
and then you have these portals, these

00:46:11.205 --> 00:46:14.055
tubes or these channels as they're
called, these aquaporin channels where

00:46:14.055 --> 00:46:15.825
water molecules can move very quickly.

00:46:16.425 --> 00:46:21.255
Now, the reason why biology seems to
have created these aquaporin channels,

00:46:21.255 --> 00:46:25.425
and again, I wasn't consulted the design
phase, but the most logical explanation

00:46:25.875 --> 00:46:32.235
is that we have many tissues within our
body that often need water very quickly

00:46:32.655 --> 00:46:35.775
or need to release water very quickly.

00:46:36.075 --> 00:46:38.985
Let's think about a couple of these
and then let's look at what the

00:46:38.985 --> 00:46:42.195
actual distribution of aquaporin
channels is throughout the body.

00:46:42.915 --> 00:46:47.625
What is an area of your body that
on occasion will need to move

00:46:47.625 --> 00:46:49.245
water very quickly out of it?

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:53.100
You can use your imagination here, but
I'll just tell you that for instance,

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:57.810
your tear glands or tear ducts
need to release tears very quickly.

00:46:57.815 --> 00:47:01.650
So you need to take water that's stored
in your body if there's an emotional

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:07.740
experience or if you look at a very bright
light, for instance or you know, God

00:47:07.745 --> 00:47:11.190
forbid if you get some sort of irritant in
your eye, you're gonna start to tear up.

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:14.759
And those tears are the release
of fluid from those tear ducts.

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:20.130
And so it's gonna be the very rapid
release of water from those tear ducts

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:21.840
through so-called aquaporin channels.

00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:24.180
And in fact, aquaporin
channels are heavily expressed.

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:27.360
There are many of them in the
cells of the so-called lacrimal

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:29.220
glands that release tears.

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:34.980
In addition, we need to
absorb water from the gut.

00:47:35.370 --> 00:47:39.184
And the gut has a lining, endothelial
lining and other cell lining and

00:47:39.185 --> 00:47:44.190
mucosal lining and water needs
often to move very quickly from our

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:46.350
stomach into the rest of the body.

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.920
And one way that is accomplished
is through aquaporin channels that

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:51.750
are expressed all along your gut.

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:55.920
So, the discovery of these aquaporin
channels is really highly significant

00:47:55.920 --> 00:47:59.759
in terms of understanding the different
ways that water can interact with

00:47:59.759 --> 00:48:01.860
and get into the cells of your body.

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:06.090
Now, there are aquaporin channels,
not just in the lacrimal glands that

00:48:06.095 --> 00:48:10.230
allow for tearing or within the gut,
but in many tissues within your body.

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:13.050
And there even have different
distributions within those tissues.

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:16.680
In fact, as one looks at the
expression of the different aquaporin

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:18.630
channels, cause it turns out
there are different forms of them.

00:48:19.635 --> 00:48:21.675
Across all the cells
and tissues of the body.

00:48:21.735 --> 00:48:25.395
There's really no single tissue
within the body, except perhaps

00:48:25.395 --> 00:48:30.225
the bones of your body and perhaps
the ligaments to some extent, that

00:48:30.225 --> 00:48:31.875
don't have these aquaporin channels.

00:48:32.235 --> 00:48:34.545
Some of you out there may have
heard of the so-called fascia,

00:48:34.550 --> 00:48:36.465
fascia and sheath muscles.

00:48:37.035 --> 00:48:41.355
They're unique kind of connective
tissue that gives some pliability

00:48:41.355 --> 00:48:45.225
and yet some rigidity that allow
for a lot of the physical abilities

00:48:45.225 --> 00:48:46.575
of your musculoskeletal system.

00:48:46.575 --> 00:48:47.475
It's incredible tissue.

00:48:47.475 --> 00:48:49.425
We'll do an entire episode
about fascia at some point.

00:48:49.425 --> 00:48:53.865
Fascinating, fascinating tissue fascia,
even contained aquaporin channels.

00:48:54.165 --> 00:48:58.515
So the role of aquaporin channels
in fascia probably relates to

00:48:58.705 --> 00:49:02.745
our specific needs to be able
to use specific muscle groups in

00:49:02.745 --> 00:49:04.545
particular ways at particular times.

00:49:04.965 --> 00:49:07.875
In other words, if you're sleeping
or lying down or sitting, you're not

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:11.115
using your musculoskeletal system
as much as if you're running or

00:49:11.115 --> 00:49:12.585
performing some repetitive behavior.

00:49:12.585 --> 00:49:15.765
It turns out that the aquaporin channels
in certain tissues, like the fascia

00:49:15.945 --> 00:49:21.005
can be used when we transition from
low mobility states to high mobility

00:49:21.010 --> 00:49:25.005
states, allowing more perfusion
or access of water into particular

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:26.355
cells of the body when we need it.

00:49:26.355 --> 00:49:29.100
Such as fascinating, fascinating channels.

00:49:29.115 --> 00:49:32.175
These aquaporin channels, and again,
only discovered fairly recently,

00:49:32.175 --> 00:49:35.385
so we're still learning new things
about our biology all the time.

00:49:35.955 --> 00:49:41.505
Now, in a very important feature of
the Aquaporin channel is that the

00:49:41.965 --> 00:49:46.500
movement of water molecules across the
cell membrane through those aquaporin

00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:51.360
channels is strongly dependent on the
temperature of water and the pH of water.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:55.410
This becomes especially important in
our description and our deep dive into

00:49:55.950 --> 00:50:00.150
so-called alkaline water or higher
pH water a little bit later, but I'll

00:50:00.155 --> 00:50:03.480
just give you a little teaser for
now because I'm sure that a number of

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.010
people are, are wondering about this.

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:10.920
If you go into the store or even a
convenient store, you will see pH water.

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:13.290
Now, every water has a pH, right?

00:50:13.295 --> 00:50:15.090
Lower numbers mean more acidic.

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:17.340
Higher numbers mean more
alkaline, or more basic.

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:19.290
You'll see pH water.

00:50:19.650 --> 00:50:21.210
That is 7.4.

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:23.580
You'll see 7.8, you'll see 9.8.

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:26.970
You'll see a huge range of these things,
and there are many, many different claims

00:50:26.970 --> 00:50:31.290
about how the pH of water is important
for regulating the pH of the body.

00:50:31.890 --> 00:50:34.050
Here's the real story.

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:39.030
The pH of your body, that is
the pH of the cells at different

00:50:39.030 --> 00:50:43.920
locations in your body, is strongly,
strongly homeostatically regulated.

00:50:44.340 --> 00:50:45.150
What do I mean by that?

00:50:45.330 --> 00:50:51.120
It means it doesn't change that much,
which means that you have very specific

00:50:51.125 --> 00:50:56.460
biological mechanisms that ensure the
pH is maintained, for instance, in the

00:50:56.460 --> 00:51:01.740
skin cells of your skin, in the retinal
cells of your eye, in your brain cells.

00:51:02.280 --> 00:51:07.080
Now, It is true that across the
body, different cells and tissues

00:51:07.080 --> 00:51:10.049
have fairly widely varying pH.

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:13.440
You know, it has been said that
the pH of bodily tissues is

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:15.840
generally between 7.2 and 7.4.

00:51:16.650 --> 00:51:21.450
However, if you were to look at the
pH of your gut, and keep in mind that

00:51:21.450 --> 00:51:26.670
your gut is not just your stomach, your
gut is the entire pathway ranging from

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:31.770
your throat all the way down to where
you excrete things out of your body.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:37.140
That entire pathway has different
pH levels depending on where you are

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:39.060
along the gut and intestinal pathway.

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:42.900
And in fact, having much lower that
is more acidic pH at certain locations

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:47.939
along your gut pathway is what allows
those gut microbiota, those little

00:51:47.939 --> 00:51:51.210
microorganisms of which you have trillions
that are important for regulating

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:54.120
everything from neurotransmitter
production to hormone production that

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:55.980
allow them to flourish and do well.

00:51:56.970 --> 00:52:04.290
That said, except under conditions of
hemorrhage or changes in blood volume

00:52:04.290 --> 00:52:08.580
that are of a dangerous level that can
lead to seizure or even death, the pH

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:13.620
of the rest of the cells of your body,
and also those gut cells doesn't change

00:52:13.799 --> 00:52:16.049
that much on a moment to moment basis.

00:52:16.470 --> 00:52:21.240
So if somebody tells you that you
should drink alkaline water or alkalized

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:26.535
water as it's sometimes called, In
order to keep your body more alkaline

00:52:26.535 --> 00:52:32.265
and less acidic, there is essentially
no basis for that at a macroscopic

00:52:32.265 --> 00:52:34.155
level or even at a local level.

00:52:34.905 --> 00:52:39.645
Now, what that does not mean is
that the pH of the water that

00:52:39.645 --> 00:52:40.995
you drink is not important.

00:52:41.265 --> 00:52:46.605
In fact, if the pH of the water that
you drink is too low, that is if the

00:52:46.605 --> 00:52:52.665
water that you consume is too acidic,
it will not move as quickly from your

00:52:52.665 --> 00:52:57.675
gut into the other regions of your
body, and therefore, the other cells of

00:52:57.680 --> 00:53:01.305
your body that require that water will
not be able to access it as readily.

00:53:01.755 --> 00:53:05.655
You've probably experienced this if you've
consumed certain water and it feels like

00:53:05.655 --> 00:53:10.515
it's sloshing around in your stomach or
it feels like it's just somehow staying

00:53:10.515 --> 00:53:14.865
there, or you feel it, its presence
more, not just as volume, but it's almost

00:53:14.865 --> 00:53:18.225
as if you can feel the little waves
of water along the inside of your gut.

00:53:18.225 --> 00:53:20.835
Now, sometimes that can relate to
temperature, but oftentimes that

00:53:20.835 --> 00:53:25.935
can relate to the pH of that water,
and it turns out it is true that

00:53:25.935 --> 00:53:32.685
water that is more alkaline, that is
pHs of 7.4 or higher can move more

00:53:32.685 --> 00:53:35.355
readily across the Aquaporin channel.

00:53:35.745 --> 00:53:39.795
And in terms of absorption of water
from the endothelial lining and

00:53:39.795 --> 00:53:43.680
the other cell type lining of your
gut into the rest of your body.

00:53:44.190 --> 00:53:49.170
It is true that higher pH water provided
that pH isn't too high, is going to be

00:53:49.175 --> 00:53:53.250
absorbed more quickly, which partially
explains why some people have an

00:53:53.250 --> 00:53:55.290
affinity for this higher pH water.

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:58.500
Now, this is not to say that you
need to consume high pH water in

00:53:58.500 --> 00:54:00.330
order to hydrate your body properly.

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:01.560
I want to be very clear about that.

00:54:01.740 --> 00:54:06.810
However, if you are interested
in what the value of elevated pH

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:11.790
water is, it largely has to do
with this accelerated absorption.

00:54:11.850 --> 00:54:15.210
And as we'll talk about a little bit
later, there is also growing evidence

00:54:15.450 --> 00:54:18.990
that it can adjust the function of
certain cells that are within your

00:54:18.990 --> 00:54:23.490
immune system and thereby reduce
certain inflammatory responses.

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:27.270
So I realize as I'm saying this, some
people out there will probably think, oh

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:30.120
no, this guy's like a pH water proponent.

00:54:30.125 --> 00:54:34.140
He's saying we have to drink alkaline
water or buy very fancy water.

00:54:34.230 --> 00:54:37.260
Now, I want to be clear that is not
what I'm saying, and I'm also not saying

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:41.880
that you need to purchase very expensive
water in order to derive the maximum

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:43.530
benefits from the water that you drank.

00:54:43.530 --> 00:54:47.040
It turns out there are a few things that
you can do by way of temperature and by

00:54:47.045 --> 00:54:50.370
way of filtering water and a few other
tricks that I'll tell you a little bit

00:54:50.370 --> 00:54:54.630
later that will allow you to increase
the absorption rate of water in the gut,

00:54:54.630 --> 00:54:59.310
which turns out to be a very interesting,
but also potentially important thing to

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:03.210
do for not just reducing inflammation,
but also making sure that you're getting

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:08.010
proper hydration of different cell types
in your body, including rapid hydration

00:55:08.010 --> 00:55:11.730
of your brain cells, which as we'll
also talk about in a few moments, can

00:55:11.730 --> 00:55:13.530
greatly enhance cognitive function.

00:55:13.890 --> 00:55:16.650
Okay, so we've talked about
how water can get into cells.

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:19.530
There are two ways, diffusion and
movement through these aquaporin channels.

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:24.150
We've earmarked the discussion that
the temperature and the pH of water,

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:26.040
that is the confirmation of water.

00:55:26.045 --> 00:55:29.430
And here I really want to embed this in
people's minds, that when we talk about

00:55:29.610 --> 00:55:34.800
temperature of water and pH of water,
we're really talking about the arrangement

00:55:34.830 --> 00:55:36.780
of those H₂O, those water molecules.

00:55:36.780 --> 00:55:37.590
So keep that in mind.

00:55:38.190 --> 00:55:40.380
We've covered how water can get
into cells through those two

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:42.600
different ways, diffusion and
through the aquaporin channels.

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:47.370
What we haven't talked about is what
happens to water once it's in cells,

00:55:47.730 --> 00:55:49.740
and this is very simple to explain.

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:54.210
Once water is inside of cells, it's
going to be incorporated into the

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:56.040
different proteins and organelles.

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:58.800
Again, organelles are things like
mitochondria, the nucleus of the cells,

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:02.550
which is contained to the DNA and so
forth in different ways, depending

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:06.870
on which proteins are there and how
hydrophilic or hydrophobic those

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:10.410
proteins are, or in some case aren't.

00:56:11.850 --> 00:56:18.705
That's an entire landscape of  protein
to water specific interactions, none of

00:56:18.705 --> 00:56:23.025
which we need to go into in any specific
detail now, but the one thing that we do

00:56:23.025 --> 00:56:28.365
need to realize and keep in mind as we
go forward is that many of the biological

00:56:28.370 --> 00:56:33.165
processes in our body that involve the
movement to molecules such as water and

00:56:33.165 --> 00:56:39.375
interactions with proteins, are going to
involve the bonding or lack of bonding

00:56:39.375 --> 00:56:41.685
between water molecules and proteins.

00:56:41.745 --> 00:56:45.615
And anytime we're talking about bonding
of one thing to the next at the level

00:56:45.620 --> 00:56:49.335
of chemistry or biology for that matter,
because they're really the same thing.

00:56:50.385 --> 00:56:54.315
We're talking about whether or not
there are electrons present or whether

00:56:54.315 --> 00:56:59.055
or not there are charges that are
opposite or the same and on and on.

00:57:00.675 --> 00:57:05.055
If you've ever heard of so-called
reactive oxygen species, what are

00:57:05.165 --> 00:57:11.265
ROS or reactive oxygen species or
so-called free radicals or antioxidants?

00:57:11.835 --> 00:57:15.495
All of that is really just
describing the presence or absence

00:57:15.735 --> 00:57:18.195
of charges that are bound or unbound.

00:57:18.195 --> 00:57:22.365
So for instance, if you hear about free
radicals, sounds pretty wild, right?

00:57:22.370 --> 00:57:23.085
Free radicals.

00:57:23.085 --> 00:57:23.985
What are free radicals?

00:57:23.985 --> 00:57:26.275
Free radicals can damage cells.

00:57:26.565 --> 00:57:30.855
They don't always damage cells, but
they can damage cells because they

00:57:30.855 --> 00:57:32.385
are essentially free electrons.

00:57:32.385 --> 00:57:36.205
They are a charge that's sitting
out there not bound to anything

00:57:36.345 --> 00:57:41.535
and therefore can interact with
the molecular structure of certain

00:57:41.535 --> 00:57:45.316
proteins and change those structures
by binding to them or interfering with

00:57:45.321 --> 00:57:50.355
the normal binding processes of those
proteins to water or to other things.

00:57:50.955 --> 00:57:55.875
And in that way cause damage to those
proteins and potentially damage to cells.

00:57:55.875 --> 00:57:59.295
Now fortunately, our cells have ways
to deal with those free radicals,

00:57:59.384 --> 00:58:01.845
and those are called antioxidants.

00:58:02.145 --> 00:58:06.465
Antioxidants are molecules that
can arrive in different forms.

00:58:06.470 --> 00:58:10.005
Sometimes we think of antioxidants as
vitamins, but they are also present in

00:58:10.005 --> 00:58:17.475
other things as well that essentially
bind up those free radicals or repair the

00:58:17.495 --> 00:58:22.395
bonds between cells so that the proteins
are no longer undergoing these, let's

00:58:22.395 --> 00:58:26.595
just call them bad confirmations, that
damage the functioning of our cells.

00:58:26.835 --> 00:58:28.904
So there are many different
theories of aging.

00:58:28.904 --> 00:58:32.865
There are many different theories of
disease, but there is not a single

00:58:32.865 --> 00:58:39.240
disease, either of brain or body that
doesn't in some way involve the generation

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:43.590
of what are called reactive oxygen
species, these free radicals and the

00:58:43.590 --> 00:58:46.950
damaging of cells at the level of their
individual organelles and so forth.

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:51.360
Nor is there a single disease of brain or
body that has not been shown to benefit

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:58.170
from having some antioxidant interference
get in the way of that oxidative process.

00:58:58.470 --> 00:59:00.780
So I realize today is pretty
thick with nomenclature.

00:59:01.440 --> 00:59:03.840
For those of you that haven't
already realized it, what you're

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:05.720
learning here is organic chemistry.

00:59:06.000 --> 00:59:09.390
So you can feel pretty good about the
fact that if you can understand the water

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:14.580
molecule and understand a little bit about
what free electron is, which is basically

00:59:14.580 --> 00:59:18.840
a charge that's out there that can
potentially do damage and the interactions

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:23.205
of things like reactive oxygen species
and the ability of, of stable bonds to

00:59:23.665 --> 00:59:28.980
buffer against  or repair certain damage
to cells as we're describing it here.

00:59:29.250 --> 00:59:31.440
Well then what you're essentially
thinking about and what we're

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:32.880
talking about is organic chemistry.

00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:36.810
Now, since this is a discussion about
chemistry as a service to try and

00:59:36.810 --> 00:59:42.930
understand the biological effects of
water, keep in mind that water itself,

00:59:43.140 --> 00:59:48.390
believe it or not, can act as an
antioxidant, provided that it's bonding to

00:59:48.390 --> 00:59:51.960
things in the proper way, which requires
that it get into cells in the proper

00:59:52.050 --> 00:59:56.570
amounts and rates, which requires that
the temperature and pH of that water be

00:59:57.690 --> 01:00:03.690
correct and provided that there's enough
water there and that that water isn't

01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:06.960
bound to other things, it's not containing
salutes that are damaging and potentially

01:00:07.260 --> 01:00:12.180
that it's carrying some of the good things
such as sodium or that there's potassium

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:15.550
present again, the so-called electrolytes
that allow cells to function well.

01:00:15.920 --> 01:00:19.800
Okay, so that's a bit of a trench of
information, and I don't want people

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:21.300
to get overwhelmed or confused.

01:00:21.690 --> 01:00:24.840
What I'm trying to do here is paint
a picture of the biology of water,

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:29.310
understanding that when you ingest
water, drinking it down, or when you

01:00:29.310 --> 01:00:32.640
breathe water vapors in the steam
room or on a humid day, that water is

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:36.180
entering your system, it's accessing
your cells through these two mechanisms,

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:39.930
diffusion across cell membranes or
movement through aquaporin channels.

01:00:39.960 --> 01:00:43.560
And then once inside those cells,
it's able to interact with and

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:47.550
change the confirmation of different
proteins and accelerate or slow

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:49.930
down different cellular reactions.

01:00:49.935 --> 01:00:55.770
Everything from normal metabolism to
blood pressure to damaged cells, depending

01:00:55.770 --> 01:00:59.970
on a number of different features of
that water, as well as what the cells

01:00:59.970 --> 01:01:01.350
happen to be doing at any given moment.

01:01:02.370 --> 01:01:07.230
So with that in mind, I'd like to turn our
attention to how water, depending on its

01:01:07.230 --> 01:01:12.060
temperature, its pH, how much we drink,
or how little we drink when we drink

01:01:12.060 --> 01:01:18.510
that water, etc., how that can impact the
health, disease and repair of different

01:01:18.510 --> 01:01:20.430
cells, tissues, and organs of our body.

