01:02:38.640 --> 01:02:43.830
Let's talk about how much water, or more
generally speaking, how much fluid each

01:02:43.830 --> 01:02:49.110
and all of us should drink each day,
and how much fluid to drink depending on

01:02:49.110 --> 01:02:51.270
our specific activities and environment.

01:02:51.660 --> 01:02:55.110
Now, this is perhaps the most
commonly asked question when

01:02:55.110 --> 01:02:57.630
the topic of water comes up, how
much water do I need to drink?

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:00.780
The other thing that comes up is
a question, which is, can't we

01:03:00.780 --> 01:03:03.540
just follow our natural thirst?

01:03:03.540 --> 01:03:06.270
That is, can't we just pay
attention to when we're thirsty?

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:07.620
And then drink fluids?

01:03:07.620 --> 01:03:10.860
And then that leads to the other
question, which is, does the color of

01:03:10.860 --> 01:03:15.090
our urine provide any indication as to
whether or not we are under hydrated,

01:03:15.095 --> 01:03:17.580
over hydrated, or hydrating correctly?

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:20.310
So let me answer each of
these things one at a time.

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:25.800
And in the backdrop, I want to highlight
the fact that there are many, many, if

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:31.800
not dozens, hundreds of studies pointing
to the fact that when we are dehydrated,

01:03:32.400 --> 01:03:35.640
our brain doesn't function as well
and our body doesn't function as well.

01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:39.630
So what I'm attempting to do in that
statement is throw a net around the

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:43.920
enormous number of studies that have
shown that even a slight state of

01:03:43.920 --> 01:03:50.250
dehydration, even 2% dehydration, can
lead to a significant and meaningful

01:03:50.250 --> 01:03:54.990
impact, that is a negative impact
on our ability to, for instance,

01:03:55.320 --> 01:03:57.240
carry out endurance type behaviors.

01:03:57.420 --> 01:04:01.500
So our ability to run on a
treadmill and stop at the point

01:04:01.500 --> 01:04:04.920
where we feel we can't continue is
going to be negatively impacted.

01:04:05.160 --> 01:04:10.380
That is we will be able to perform
less work for less period of time

01:04:10.530 --> 01:04:12.480
when we are even slightly dehydrated.

01:04:12.510 --> 01:04:16.020
Likewise, our strength is reduced
by even slight dehydration.

01:04:16.110 --> 01:04:22.130
Likewise, our cognitive performance,
including memory, focus, creative

01:04:22.135 --> 01:04:26.730
thinking, flexible thinking of different
kinds, are all significantly impaired

01:04:26.730 --> 01:04:28.620
when we are in states of dehydration.

01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:32.430
Now, that raises an additional question
that deserves attention, which is how

01:04:32.430 --> 01:04:34.080
do we actually measure dehydration?

01:04:34.200 --> 01:04:37.410
Now you hear different things like if you
pinch the skin on the top of your hand and

01:04:37.410 --> 01:04:42.180
it takes more than three seconds to lay
down again flat, then you're dehydrated.

01:04:42.185 --> 01:04:47.340
You hear that, you hear, okay, if you
are to press on your fingernail and see

01:04:47.340 --> 01:04:51.840
a change in the color of the tissue,
just be below your fingernail, which

01:04:51.840 --> 01:04:56.970
indeed does happen, and it does not go
back to its original color within one to

01:04:56.970 --> 01:04:58.500
three seconds, then you're dehydrated.

01:04:58.500 --> 01:05:01.470
You hear things like this if your ankles
are swollen when you're wearing socks,

01:05:01.470 --> 01:05:06.080
you take off the socks and you can see
the imprint of the socks on your lower

01:05:06.080 --> 01:05:07.290
limbs, that means you're dehydrated.

01:05:07.290 --> 01:05:09.811
You hear this kind of stuff,
and you should probably be

01:05:09.816 --> 01:05:11.040
wondering, is any of that true?

01:05:11.670 --> 01:05:15.840
To some extent, it is true, although
it can vary quite a bit by how

01:05:15.840 --> 01:05:19.320
old you are, whether or not  the
skin on the top of your hand tends

01:05:19.320 --> 01:05:22.710
to be looser or not depending on
whether or not you're leaner or not.

01:05:22.890 --> 01:05:26.220
So in other words, those
are not absolutely objective

01:05:26.220 --> 01:05:27.420
measures of dehydration.

01:05:27.660 --> 01:05:31.890
Now, it is true that if normally you can
pinch the skin on the top of your hand

01:05:31.890 --> 01:05:35.730
and it returns to its normal flattened
position within about one to two or three

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:41.910
seconds, and it does not do that within
five or more seconds, there's a decent

01:05:41.910 --> 01:05:44.490
probability that you're a little bit
dehydrated, that you need to ingest some

01:05:44.490 --> 01:05:49.890
fluid, or that if you press down on your
nail and you see the depression causes a

01:05:50.100 --> 01:05:55.350
transition from kind of a pink color to a
white color, and then you release and it

01:05:55.350 --> 01:06:00.600
doesn't go back to its original pinkish
color within a few seconds, well then

01:06:00.840 --> 01:06:02.050
there's a chance that you're dehydrated.

01:06:02.070 --> 01:06:04.410
But again, these are not
perfect measures of dehydration.

01:06:04.890 --> 01:06:08.520
You may be surprised to learn, and I
was surprised to learn that most of

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:14.430
the basis for these statements, like
even a 2% dehydration state, can lead

01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:18.540
to significant reductions in cognitive
or physical performance are based on

01:06:18.720 --> 01:06:24.630
not direct measures of hydration, but
rather on measures of reductions in water

01:06:24.630 --> 01:06:27.120
intake, which is a different thing, right?

01:06:27.270 --> 01:06:32.955
It's saying that ordinarily, a person
of a given body weight needs X amount

01:06:32.955 --> 01:06:38.475
of fluid per day, and when they get even
just 2% less than that amount of fluid

01:06:38.714 --> 01:06:43.035
than their cognitive and or physical
performance is impaired, rather than

01:06:43.035 --> 01:06:45.705
focusing on dehydration of tissues.

01:06:46.035 --> 01:06:46.190
Okay?

01:06:46.190 --> 01:06:48.975
Now that might seem like a
subtle distinction, but it's

01:06:49.335 --> 01:06:51.105
actually a meaningful distinction
when you think about it.

01:06:51.285 --> 01:06:54.575
However, it's a meaningful
distinction that we can leverage

01:06:54.580 --> 01:06:58.634
toward understanding how much water
or fluid we need to drink each day.

01:06:58.694 --> 01:07:02.714
Now, there we can really point to some
solid numbers that, believe it or not,

01:07:02.774 --> 01:07:04.665
are fairly independent of body weight.

01:07:04.725 --> 01:07:06.944
Now, I say independent of body weight.

01:07:07.395 --> 01:07:13.125
I'm referring to the amount of fluid
that most healthy adults need at rest.

01:07:13.185 --> 01:07:14.174
What do I mean by at rest?

01:07:14.174 --> 01:07:18.645
I mean when not exercising and when
not in extremely hot environments.

01:07:18.645 --> 01:07:23.355
So I'm leaving aside you a desert
ultra marathoners or people that are

01:07:24.075 --> 01:07:28.845
doing any kind of movement or living in
environments that are very, very hot.

01:07:29.835 --> 01:07:33.944
Here, I'm mainly referring to people that
live most of their daily life in indoor

01:07:33.944 --> 01:07:37.725
environments, could be air conditioned or
not air conditioned, heated or not heated.

01:07:37.905 --> 01:07:41.265
What we're trying to arrive at here are
some numbers that can work across the

01:07:41.265 --> 01:07:45.405
board, because of course there are an
infinite number of different conditions

01:07:45.405 --> 01:07:47.265
that each and all of you are existing in.

01:07:47.445 --> 01:07:52.335
So I'm not going to attempt to give you a
body weight by activity, by environment,

01:07:52.340 --> 01:07:54.835
by humidity formula calculation.

01:07:54.840 --> 01:07:56.985
In fact, no such calculation exists.

01:07:57.195 --> 01:08:02.595
However, there are formulas that can
put you into very stable frameworks.

01:08:02.595 --> 01:08:08.685
That is levels of water intake for
periods of rest when you're not exercising

01:08:08.745 --> 01:08:14.355
and for when you are exercising, that
will ensure that you are hydrating

01:08:14.835 --> 01:08:19.995
with the one exception being if you
are exercising or if you are living in

01:08:19.995 --> 01:08:23.415
very, very hot conditions and you're
not heat adapted to those conditions.

01:08:23.625 --> 01:08:24.735
So what are those numbers?

01:08:24.735 --> 01:08:27.585
In other words, what is the
answer to the question of how

01:08:27.590 --> 01:08:29.025
much fluid do we need each day?

01:08:29.030 --> 01:08:30.435
And here I'm referring to fluid.

01:08:30.435 --> 01:08:33.795
I'm not distinguishing between
water, caffeinated beverages,

01:08:33.855 --> 01:08:35.055
soda, tea, and so on.

01:08:35.055 --> 01:08:36.255
I'll discuss that in a moment.

01:08:37.605 --> 01:08:45.255
We can reasonably say that for every hour
that you are awake in the first 10 hours

01:08:45.255 --> 01:08:49.245
of your day, this is important, in the
first 10 hours of your day, you should

01:08:49.245 --> 01:08:53.205
consume on average, 8 ounces of fluid.

01:08:53.805 --> 01:08:58.765
Now, for those of you that are using
the metric system, not ounces, 8

01:08:58.785 --> 01:09:03.705
ounces of fluid is approximately
236 milliliters of water.

01:09:04.425 --> 01:09:07.245
And for those of you that exist in
the metric system and aren't used to

01:09:07.245 --> 01:09:11.955
thinking about ounces and vice versa,
just think about a typical can of soda.

01:09:12.075 --> 01:09:14.055
In the United States, it's 12 ounces.

01:09:14.895 --> 01:09:17.595
In Europe, sometimes the cans of
soda are a little bit smaller, but

01:09:17.595 --> 01:09:19.215
that's a whole discussion unto itself.

01:09:19.485 --> 01:09:26.715
But 8 ounces of fluid, that is 236,
let's just say 240 milliliters because

01:09:26.745 --> 01:09:32.475
we don't need to be too precise here,
of fluid on average every hour for

01:09:32.475 --> 01:09:36.585
the first 10 hours of your day, which
translates to an average of 80 ounces

01:09:36.585 --> 01:09:43.695
of fluid for the first 10 hours of your
day, or 2,360 milliliters of water.

01:09:44.715 --> 01:09:48.855
In other words, approximately 2
liters of water plus a little bit more

01:09:49.425 --> 01:09:51.495
for the first 10 hours of your day.

01:09:51.915 --> 01:09:55.605
Now, I wanna be very clear that
this does not mean that you need to

01:09:55.815 --> 01:10:00.615
ingest 8 ounces or 236 milliliters
of fluid on the hour, every hour

01:10:00.615 --> 01:10:01.815
for the first 10 hours of your day.

01:10:01.820 --> 01:10:02.895
I'm certainly not saying that.

01:10:03.225 --> 01:10:06.285
And in fact, most people are
going to find that they're

01:10:06.285 --> 01:10:08.505
going to ingest water in bolus.

01:10:08.505 --> 01:10:13.425
That is, they're gonna have perhaps
16 ounces of water, 500 milliliters

01:10:13.425 --> 01:10:16.635
of water at one portion of the day,
and then maybe a couple hours of later

01:10:16.635 --> 01:10:20.545
that they'll drink some more water or
some more coffee or soda or some other

01:10:20.545 --> 01:10:22.995
beverage and another portion of the day.

01:10:23.685 --> 01:10:27.585
I do think, however, it's important for
most of us to take a step back and ask

01:10:27.585 --> 01:10:31.125
ourselves whether or not independent
of any other activity or environmental

01:10:31.125 --> 01:10:37.755
conditions, whether or not we are in
fact ingesting 80 ounces or basically

01:10:38.055 --> 01:10:45.015
2.4 liters of water for that 10 hours
of the day that spans from the time

01:10:45.015 --> 01:10:47.835
we wake up until 10 hours later.

01:10:48.225 --> 01:10:50.415
Now, why am I setting
this 10 hour framework?

01:10:51.045 --> 01:10:54.345
The reason I'm setting this 10 hour
framework is that it turns out that

01:10:54.345 --> 01:10:58.725
your fluid requirements, even just
at rest, are vastly different in

01:10:58.725 --> 01:11:03.795
the time from when you wake up until
about 10 hours later, as compared

01:11:03.795 --> 01:11:05.685
to the later evening and nighttime.

01:11:05.685 --> 01:11:08.055
And here I'm referring to people
that are not doing night shifts.

01:11:08.235 --> 01:11:12.285
But if you are requesting a number of
how much fluid to drink independent

01:11:12.285 --> 01:11:16.875
of our needs for fluid for exercise,
that's going to be 8 ounces of fluid

01:11:16.875 --> 01:11:22.935
or 240 milliliters of fluid on average
for every hour from the time when

01:11:22.935 --> 01:11:24.665
we wake up until 10 hours later.

01:11:25.165 --> 01:11:29.040
That's the simple formulation that
should basically ensure that you're

01:11:29.040 --> 01:11:33.419
getting sufficient baseline hydration
for the cells and tissues of your body.

01:11:33.780 --> 01:11:38.160
Now, if you are engaging in exercise,
whether or not it's endurance exercise or

01:11:38.165 --> 01:11:41.820
whether or not it's resistance training
exercise, you are going to need additional

01:11:41.825 --> 01:11:47.879
fluids in order to maximize the effects
of that exercise and to avoid dehydration.

01:11:48.150 --> 01:11:51.179
And there too, we have some excellent
numbers that we can look to.

01:11:51.610 --> 01:11:53.700
Excellent, because they
arrive from research.

01:11:53.879 --> 01:11:57.929
And this is largely peeled from
the episode that I did with Dr.

01:11:57.929 --> 01:12:00.389
Andy Galpin, professor of
Kinesiology at Cal State Fullerton.

01:12:00.389 --> 01:12:03.570
We did a six episode series all about
exercise, everything from strength

01:12:03.570 --> 01:12:06.719
training, hypertrophy, endurance,
nutrition, supplementation, recovery,

01:12:06.719 --> 01:12:08.009
everything related to exercise.

01:12:08.309 --> 01:12:10.070
You can find all of
that at hubermanlab.com.

01:12:10.085 --> 01:12:13.950
And one of the components of those
episodes that was discussed, but that

01:12:13.955 --> 01:12:18.719
some of you may have not heard, is
that there is a simple formula for

01:12:18.724 --> 01:12:20.759
how much fluid to ingest on average.

01:12:20.759 --> 01:12:25.710
Keep in mind, this is on average when
you are exercising, and I refer to

01:12:25.710 --> 01:12:28.349
this as the so-called Galpin equation.

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:33.240
The Galpin equation states that
you should take your body weight in

01:12:33.245 --> 01:12:38.160
pounds, divide that by 30, and that
will give you the number of ounces

01:12:38.160 --> 01:12:43.620
of fluid to ingest every 15 to 20
minutes on average while exercising.

01:12:44.205 --> 01:12:50.715
Okay, your body weight impounds divided by
30 equals the number of ounces of fluid to

01:12:50.715 --> 01:12:53.115
consume on average every 15 to 20 minutes.

01:12:53.175 --> 01:12:56.385
When I say on average, what I mean
is it is not the case that you

01:12:56.385 --> 01:13:01.215
need to stop every 15 or 20 minutes
and consume that volume of fluid.

01:13:01.455 --> 01:13:03.405
You could sip it from moment to moment.

01:13:03.675 --> 01:13:07.545
You could wait half an hour or
an hour and then consume a larger

01:13:07.545 --> 01:13:08.925
bolus of fluid, a larger amount.

01:13:10.105 --> 01:13:14.985
Although it is recommended for
performance sake that you sip

01:13:15.105 --> 01:13:20.115
or consume beverages fairly
consistently throughout your training.

01:13:20.415 --> 01:13:23.085
One's ability to do that is going
to depend on a number of things like

01:13:23.085 --> 01:13:26.506
gastric emptying time, whether or
not the particular exercise you're

01:13:26.510 --> 01:13:29.865
doing, whether or not it's running
or jumping, is compatible with

01:13:29.865 --> 01:13:31.965
ingesting fluid on a regular basis.

01:13:31.965 --> 01:13:34.155
Or whether or not you need to
do it at different intervals

01:13:34.155 --> 01:13:35.385
than every 15, 20 minutes.

01:13:35.385 --> 01:13:37.575
Maybe it's every 5 minutes,
maybe it's every half hour.

01:13:37.935 --> 01:13:39.015
You have to adjust for you.

01:13:39.015 --> 01:13:43.185
But if you were to take the hour of
exercise or the half hour of exercise

01:13:43.635 --> 01:13:48.644
or the 3 hours of exercise and ask how
much fluid to ingest, it's going to be

01:13:48.644 --> 01:13:51.495
that Galpin equation of body weight and
pounds to buy it by 30 equals the number

01:13:51.495 --> 01:13:53.175
of ounces for every 15 or 20 minutes.

01:13:53.565 --> 01:13:55.905
And of course, I can already
hear screaming from the back.

01:13:56.085 --> 01:13:59.144
What about for those of us
who follow the metric system?

01:13:59.144 --> 01:14:02.505
And there there's a simple translation
of the Galpin equation, which

01:14:02.505 --> 01:14:08.035
is that you need approximately 2
milliliters of water per kilogram of

01:14:08.035 --> 01:14:10.155
body weight, every 15 to 20 minutes.

01:14:10.155 --> 01:14:14.485
Again, the Galpin equation converted
into the metric system is going to be 2

01:14:14.505 --> 01:14:19.215
milliliters of water per kilogram of body
weight every 15 to 20 minutes on average.

01:14:19.635 --> 01:14:22.785
I'm sure a number of you are asking
whether or not hydration prior

01:14:22.785 --> 01:14:24.225
to exercise is also important.

01:14:24.230 --> 01:14:27.315
It absolutely is, and if you follow
the numbers that I talked about before,

01:14:27.815 --> 01:14:35.865
approximately 8 ounces or 240 milliliters
of fluid intake per hour in the first 10

01:14:35.865 --> 01:14:40.605
hours of waking, that should establish a
good baseline of hydration heading into

01:14:40.610 --> 01:14:45.615
exercise, which then prompts the next
question I often get, which is, is the

01:14:45.615 --> 01:14:48.585
amount of water that needs to be consumed
according to the Galpin equation during

01:14:49.105 --> 01:14:52.625
exercise on top of, or separate from that?

01:14:52.630 --> 01:14:57.405
That is, does it replace the amount of
fluid that one needs at a basic level,

01:14:57.525 --> 01:14:59.385
that 8 ounces or 240 milliliters?

01:14:59.445 --> 01:15:02.235
And there the answer
sort of goes both ways.

01:15:02.235 --> 01:15:06.285
I think if you're going to
exercise, then obviously follow

01:15:06.285 --> 01:15:08.445
the Galpin equation in some way.

01:15:08.535 --> 01:15:10.995
Again, you don't need to be
ultra specific about this.

01:15:10.995 --> 01:15:14.415
These are ballpark figures that will
ensure hydration, so we've set them a

01:15:14.415 --> 01:15:19.075
little bit higher perhaps than needed to
ensure more hydration rather than less.

01:15:19.769 --> 01:15:23.700
But basically the short answer is if
you're exercising for about an hour,

01:15:23.700 --> 01:15:26.580
most people are exercising for an hour
or two, probably not more than that.

01:15:26.580 --> 01:15:29.309
Most of my workouts are
certainly the resistant training

01:15:29.309 --> 01:15:30.809
workouts last about an hour.

01:15:31.080 --> 01:15:35.370
Well, then you can replace the 8
ounces or the 240 milliliters of water

01:15:35.429 --> 01:15:38.940
that's required at baseline with what
you consume according to the Galpin

01:15:38.940 --> 01:15:40.760
equation during that bout of exercise.

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:46.410
A common question is if you are exercising
in a heated environment, indoor, outdoor,

01:15:46.620 --> 01:15:50.220
or you are somebody who tends to sweat
a lot, and by the way, we can all get

01:15:50.220 --> 01:15:51.720
better at sweating, by sweating more.

01:15:51.724 --> 01:15:53.040
Sweat is an adaptation.

01:15:53.099 --> 01:15:55.860
So if you sit in the sauna more,
you're gonna get better at sweating.

01:15:55.860 --> 01:15:58.920
If you exercise more, especially if
you wear more layers, or if you do

01:15:58.920 --> 01:16:01.620
it in hotter temperatures or more
humid temperatures, you're gonna

01:16:01.950 --> 01:16:03.389
get better at sweating over time.

01:16:03.389 --> 01:16:05.580
And sweating is an adaptation
that helps cool your body.

01:16:06.660 --> 01:16:10.800
If you are sweating a lot or you're in
heat, how much fluid should you ingest?

01:16:11.519 --> 01:16:17.190
In general, I think it's safe to say that
you may want to increase the values on

01:16:17.190 --> 01:16:19.580
the Galpin equation by about 50 to a 100%.

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:23.070
So either increase by 50% or double
those numbers if you're in a very hot

01:16:23.070 --> 01:16:24.929
environment or sweating an awful lot.

01:16:25.650 --> 01:16:30.330
If you are sitting in the sauna, I highly
recommend consuming at least 8 ounces and

01:16:30.759 --> 01:16:33.089
probably more like 16 ounces of fluid.

01:16:33.089 --> 01:16:37.440
So that translates again to about 240
or about 480, let's just round up 500

01:16:37.440 --> 01:16:44.700
milliliters of fluid for every 20 to
30 minutes that you are in a hot sauna.

01:16:44.730 --> 01:16:46.530
And then of course,
people ask, well, how hot?

01:16:46.530 --> 01:16:49.769
And it, okay, that starts getting
really detailed and we can't distinguish

01:16:49.775 --> 01:16:51.389
between dry saunas and wet saunas.

01:16:51.389 --> 01:16:53.160
And again, too many variables.

01:16:53.165 --> 01:16:59.669
But I would double your fluid intake
for that hot environment exercise or

01:16:59.669 --> 01:17:01.384
for that hot environment sauna sit.

01:17:02.224 --> 01:17:06.150
Also if you are feeling dehydrated,
okay, what does feeling dehydrated mean?

01:17:06.450 --> 01:17:09.330
That actually has a definition that
we can get into a little bit later.

01:17:09.330 --> 01:17:12.900
But what we're really talking about here
is if you are feeling as if your throat

01:17:12.900 --> 01:17:18.059
is dry, you are "parched", or you're very
thirsty, well then there's absolutely

01:17:18.059 --> 01:17:20.400
no problem with ingesting more fluids.

01:17:20.400 --> 01:17:24.794
So 16 ounces of fluid or 500
milliliters of fluid per hour

01:17:24.794 --> 01:17:25.905
while you're feeling parched.

01:17:25.995 --> 01:17:29.714
My read of the literature is that
thirst is a reasonable guide for

01:17:29.714 --> 01:17:32.144
when we tend to be dehydrated.

01:17:32.325 --> 01:17:36.705
However, it is the case that our
thirst doesn't really keep up with

01:17:36.709 --> 01:17:38.325
our body's level of dehydration.

01:17:38.384 --> 01:17:41.504
And we know that based on some really
nice studies that have explored the

01:17:41.504 --> 01:17:45.464
amount of fluid intake compared to the
amount of urination, compared to the

01:17:45.464 --> 01:17:49.004
amount of physical output compared to the
environment that one happens to be in.

01:17:49.605 --> 01:17:52.575
And these are sort of older
studies in the realm of physiology.

01:17:52.844 --> 01:17:55.875
But here's the basic rule of thumb
that's gonna work for most people.

01:17:56.025 --> 01:18:00.884
If you are feeling parched, consume
fluids, ideally you consume fluids that

01:18:00.884 --> 01:18:03.884
don't contain caffeine or other diuretics.

01:18:03.884 --> 01:18:09.681
Diuretics being substances that cause
the release, the urination of fluid from

01:18:09.681 --> 01:18:15.375
the body and or if you are consuming
caffeine either prior to or after bouts

01:18:15.375 --> 01:18:19.304
of exercise or even just at work or you
work in a air conditioned or otherwise

01:18:19.365 --> 01:18:24.495
dry, cool or hot environment, that
you try and include some sodium and

01:18:24.495 --> 01:18:28.094
ideally sodium, potassium, magnesium,
the electrolytes in that beverage.

01:18:28.094 --> 01:18:30.554
So it could be a little pinch
of sea salt with some lemon to

01:18:30.554 --> 01:18:31.695
adjust the taste a little bit.

01:18:31.934 --> 01:18:34.715
It could be an electrolyte drink,
an LMNT or some other sort, they're

01:18:34.715 --> 01:18:36.075
a lot of different types out there.

01:18:36.585 --> 01:18:39.974
For most people drinking pure water,
and I realize that many people do

01:18:39.974 --> 01:18:41.415
just like the taste of pure water.

01:18:42.134 --> 01:18:45.644
Chances are you're going to have enough
electrolytes unless you're sweating

01:18:45.644 --> 01:18:49.245
quite a bit or you're exercising quite
a lot, and under conditions where you

01:18:49.245 --> 01:18:52.065
are consuming very few carbohydrates,
you're going to excrete more fluid.

01:18:52.335 --> 01:18:55.995
If you are ingesting caffeine, whether
or not it's from tea or coffee, I

01:18:55.995 --> 01:19:00.735
highly recommend increasing your non
caffeine fluid intake about two to

01:19:00.735 --> 01:19:02.865
one for every volume of caffeine.

01:19:03.165 --> 01:19:07.275
So in other words, if you have a 6 ounces
or 8 ounces of coffee, you're gonna want

01:19:07.275 --> 01:19:12.495
12 to 16 ounces of fluid, ideally fluid
with electrolytes, or a little pinch of

01:19:12.495 --> 01:19:14.804
salt in order to offset that dehydration.

01:19:15.554 --> 01:19:19.184
So hopefully those will provide good
rules of thumb for what people want to do

01:19:19.184 --> 01:19:20.535
when they're just moving about their day.

01:19:20.535 --> 01:19:24.315
Again, underscored by the fact that
even slight levels of dehydration can

01:19:24.315 --> 01:19:28.875
really impair our cognitive and physical
performance largely by creating fatigue.

01:19:29.384 --> 01:19:32.925
But more often than not
by creating brain fog.

01:19:33.045 --> 01:19:34.934
You know, I get so many
questions about brain fog.

01:19:34.934 --> 01:19:35.955
Why do I have brain fog?

01:19:35.955 --> 01:19:36.795
Why do I have brain fog?

01:19:36.974 --> 01:19:41.715
There is a vast literature showing
that quality hydration, meaning

01:19:41.715 --> 01:19:45.375
hydration that matches the demands
of humidity and output as described

01:19:45.375 --> 01:19:49.755
in the equations that we went over a
little bit before, really can enhance

01:19:49.755 --> 01:19:51.855
clarity of focus and overall energy.

01:19:51.855 --> 01:19:55.215
And we'll talk about why that is, but
I'll just allude to it a little bit here.

01:19:55.245 --> 01:19:59.835
The reason why ingesting sufficient
fluids can enhance our ability to

01:19:59.835 --> 01:20:04.185
focus, and in fact can reduce brain
fog and can increase physical vigor

01:20:04.185 --> 01:20:06.435
and output is not mysterious to us.

01:20:06.795 --> 01:20:11.295
We know that there are two mechanisms
by which fluid intake triggers

01:20:11.710 --> 01:20:15.010
elevated levels of alertness, and
it all has to do with the so-called

01:20:15.010 --> 01:20:18.490
sympathetic arm of the autonomic nervous
system, which is a real mouthful.

01:20:18.670 --> 01:20:21.340
But basically the sympathetic arm
of the autonomic nervous system, as

01:20:21.340 --> 01:20:25.150
many of you've heard me talk about
before, is the aspect of your nervous

01:20:25.155 --> 01:20:26.250
system that makes you more alert.

01:20:26.250 --> 01:20:29.410
Has nothing to do with emotional sympathy,
has to do with a bunch of neurons in

01:20:29.410 --> 01:20:32.320
the middle of your spinal cord called
the sympathetic chain ganglia, and

01:20:32.320 --> 01:20:35.890
some other related neural networks
in your body, as well as regions of

01:20:35.890 --> 01:20:39.580
your brain, like the locus coeruleus,
that release things like epinephrine

01:20:39.580 --> 01:20:43.950
and norepinephrine and make you more
alert and in a kind of magnificent

01:20:44.270 --> 01:20:49.240
arrangement,  or I think magnificent
arrangement, when we have fluid in our

01:20:49.240 --> 01:20:55.600
gut and when our cells are well hydrated,
and believe it or not, when our bladder

01:20:55.600 --> 01:21:00.430
contains fluid within it, there is an
elevation in activity of the sympathetic

01:21:00.430 --> 01:21:02.710
nervous system by way of two pathways.

01:21:02.830 --> 01:21:04.000
One is mechanical.

01:21:04.060 --> 01:21:07.690
In fact, we have so-called stretch
receptors in our bladder and in our

01:21:07.690 --> 01:21:12.160
gut, these stretch receptors have
fancy names like TRP trip channels

01:21:12.160 --> 01:21:16.615
as they're called, or piezo, which
are these stretch sensing channels.

01:21:16.905 --> 01:21:19.665
This is the beautiful work of many
laboratories, but in particular

01:21:20.075 --> 01:21:23.125
David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian.

01:21:23.145 --> 01:21:27.595
David Julius is at UC San Francisco,
Ardem is at the Scripps Institute.

01:21:28.065 --> 01:21:31.395
They've discovered a bunch of
channels in cells that sends things

01:21:31.395 --> 01:21:36.685
from cold to different mechanical
pressure, including expansion of

01:21:36.685 --> 01:21:38.894
tissues so-called Mechanosensation.

01:21:39.255 --> 01:21:43.365
And basically what it all boils down
to is that when our bladder has some

01:21:43.370 --> 01:21:46.665
fluid in it, when our stomach has
some fluid in it, and when our cells

01:21:46.665 --> 01:21:50.715
are sufficiently hydrated, they send
information about the mechanical

01:21:50.715 --> 01:21:52.545
presence of that distension even.

01:21:52.545 --> 01:21:55.305
And then here, I'm not talking
about being like overly full or, you

01:21:55.305 --> 01:21:58.575
know, full of chock-a-block, full
of fluid or your bladder feeling,

01:21:58.755 --> 01:21:59.865
you know, really, really full.

01:21:59.870 --> 01:22:00.885
We'll talk about that in a moment.

01:22:01.185 --> 01:22:05.025
But when we are sufficiently hydrated,
there's a mechanical signature of

01:22:05.025 --> 01:22:07.995
that, which is the expansion of our
tissues because it has more fluid in it.

01:22:08.355 --> 01:22:12.165
And there are chemical signals as
well, which is the movement of water

01:22:12.165 --> 01:22:16.485
across those aquaporin channels is
actually understood at a biological

01:22:16.485 --> 01:22:20.955
level by ourselves and sends information
to the areas of the brain that are

01:22:20.955 --> 01:22:24.285
associated with so-called sympathetic
arousal and makes us more alert.

01:22:24.465 --> 01:22:27.255
This is actually what wakes us
up in the middle of the night.

01:22:27.945 --> 01:22:31.215
If we have consumed too much
fluid prior to sleep and we

01:22:31.215 --> 01:22:32.325
need to urinate, we wake up.

01:22:32.330 --> 01:22:37.575
This is a mechanism that is not adequately
developed in babies and young children.

01:22:37.580 --> 01:22:41.115
This is why babies, young
children often will wet their bed.

01:22:41.445 --> 01:22:46.005
And believe it or not, in both
humans and in dogs, our ability to

01:22:46.005 --> 01:22:49.005
control urination voluntarily is
something that we actually learn.

01:22:49.005 --> 01:22:50.415
Babies just pee in their diaper.

01:22:50.565 --> 01:22:54.045
Dogs just pee on the floor until
their house broken or until a child

01:22:54.045 --> 01:22:58.425
learns to hold onto their urine,
until they go to the bathroom, in the

01:22:58.835 --> 01:23:03.045
bathroom, or particularly appropriate
location outdoors or otherwise.

01:23:03.855 --> 01:23:08.715
The point is that hydration of the body
is signaled to the brain when we have

01:23:08.715 --> 01:23:12.315
enough fluid in the tissues of our body,
when we've consumed enough fluid, even

01:23:12.320 --> 01:23:16.275
if it hasn't already arrived to the cells
and tissues of our body, that is signaled

01:23:16.275 --> 01:23:17.795
to the brain in the form of alertness.

01:23:18.435 --> 01:23:23.025
And that alertness is what translates
to the enhanced cognitive abilities

01:23:23.030 --> 01:23:24.585
that we have when we are well hydrated.

01:23:24.765 --> 01:23:28.455
It's also what translates to our
enhanced physical abilities when we

01:23:28.455 --> 01:23:29.805
are challenged with physical tasks.

01:23:29.805 --> 01:23:32.355
So when you look out on the landscape
of all these studies that have

01:23:32.355 --> 01:23:36.405
shown impairments in physical or
cognitive performance under conditions

01:23:36.405 --> 01:23:40.755
of even slight dehydration, that
all makes sense because our cells

01:23:40.755 --> 01:23:42.315
need fluid and we need water.

01:23:42.375 --> 01:23:46.605
But it also prompts the question
of, well, does being well hydrated

01:23:47.035 --> 01:23:50.085
actually make our brain and body
function better in the context of

01:23:50.085 --> 01:23:51.315
physical and cognitive performance?

01:23:51.475 --> 01:23:53.175
And indeed, the answer is yes.

01:23:53.505 --> 01:23:56.295
Now, earlier we were talking about
these equations that you can apply.

01:23:56.955 --> 01:24:01.605
And here again, I really want to emphasize
that these equations were not meant to

01:24:01.605 --> 01:24:03.735
be followed down to the decimal point.

01:24:03.855 --> 01:24:09.285
They were really meant and are meant
as crude, but sufficient guides for

01:24:09.285 --> 01:24:12.705
you to make sure that you're getting
enough hydration depending on your

01:24:12.705 --> 01:24:14.144
levels of activity and at rest.

01:24:15.165 --> 01:24:18.794
If you recall when we talked about
those equations, I said, you need

01:24:18.794 --> 01:24:24.285
about 8 ounces or 240 milliliters
of fluid per hour for the first

01:24:24.285 --> 01:24:26.384
10 hours of your day after waking.

01:24:26.535 --> 01:24:28.155
Now, why did I say for the first 10 hours?

01:24:28.155 --> 01:24:32.235
Well, it turns out that the filtration
of fluids from your body, which is

01:24:32.235 --> 01:24:36.974
accomplished of course, by your kidneys
and by way of your bladder, and the

01:24:36.974 --> 01:24:42.644
excretion of fluid out urethra, so-called
urination, is strongly, strongly

01:24:42.644 --> 01:24:48.075
circadian dependent, meaning the cells
of your kidney and the cells even of

01:24:48.075 --> 01:24:51.134
your gut, in fact, all the cells of
your body, but especially the cells

01:24:51.134 --> 01:24:55.155
of your kidney, which filter the fluid
that comes into your body, and that

01:24:55.160 --> 01:24:59.294
makes certain hormones like vasopressin,
which is antidiuretic hormone.

01:24:59.804 --> 01:25:04.665
All of that functioning of the kidney
is under strong regulation by so-called

01:25:04.695 --> 01:25:07.844
circadian clock genes, circadian clock
genes are genes that are expressed

01:25:07.844 --> 01:25:11.985
in every cell, but that in certain
cells of the body very strongly

01:25:11.985 --> 01:25:15.705
impact whether or not that organ, in
this case, the kidney is going to be

01:25:15.794 --> 01:25:21.495
activated, meaning functioning at a
very high level or at a reduced level.

01:25:21.525 --> 01:25:25.455
And we can make all of this very simple
by simply stating what's contained in

01:25:25.455 --> 01:25:27.994
this beautiful review that I'll provide
a link to if you want to learn more

01:25:28.040 --> 01:25:29.625
called circadian rhythms in the kidney.

01:25:30.434 --> 01:25:34.214
And basically what is known is that
for the first 10 hours after waking,

01:25:34.245 --> 01:25:38.264
your kidney is filtering fluid within
your body at a very rapid rate.

01:25:38.445 --> 01:25:41.174
There are a number of different cell
types that do that, but they are

01:25:41.174 --> 01:25:46.605
basically taking that fluid, pulling
out any contaminants using hormones such

01:25:46.610 --> 01:25:50.295
as antidiuretic hormone, vasopressin
to adjust whether or not you're gonna

01:25:50.295 --> 01:25:53.535
hold onto fluid or release more fluid
from your body in the form of urine.

01:25:53.535 --> 01:25:57.495
Depending on the salt concentration,
depending on how much fluid

01:25:57.495 --> 01:26:01.964
you need, your work output, the
conditions you're in, all of that.

01:26:03.134 --> 01:26:08.594
However, at about 10 hours after waking
your kidney really starts to reduce

01:26:08.594 --> 01:26:10.094
its overall level of functioning.

01:26:10.094 --> 01:26:14.684
Now that doesn't mean that your kidney
cannot filter fluid 11 or 12 or 16

01:26:14.684 --> 01:26:18.344
hours after waking, but it becomes far
less efficient at doing so, and thank

01:26:18.344 --> 01:26:22.605
goodness it does because you do not
want your kidney filtering fluid at

01:26:22.804 --> 01:26:29.205
the same rate at midnight, assuming you
wake up at say, 7 or 8 or 9:00 AM that

01:26:29.205 --> 01:26:32.955
it was filtering fluid at 10:00 AM.

01:26:33.674 --> 01:26:39.285
In fact, we can say that if you want
to reduce your nighttime waking in

01:26:39.290 --> 01:26:43.214
order to urinate, which is a common,
common question and concern that many

01:26:43.214 --> 01:26:45.855
people have, how can I avoid waking up
in the middle of the night to urinate?

01:26:45.915 --> 01:26:50.625
And there I say it's perfectly normal
to wake up once, maybe twice each night

01:26:50.655 --> 01:26:54.525
to urinate, but if you want to reduce
the number of times that you wake up

01:26:54.525 --> 01:26:58.545
in order to urinate across the night,
maybe even make that number zero times.

01:26:59.415 --> 01:27:02.415
You will greatly benefit
by doing 3 things.

01:27:02.474 --> 01:27:06.945
First of all, make sure that you're
hydrating sufficiently during the daytime

01:27:07.275 --> 01:27:12.344
per the equations that we talked about
earlier, that will ensure that you are

01:27:12.344 --> 01:27:17.115
not excessively thirsty in the evening
and therefore consuming a lot more fluid.

01:27:18.105 --> 01:27:22.455
Second, and related to that first point
is that you do want to reduce your

01:27:22.455 --> 01:27:26.535
fluid intake at night, provided you
hydrated sufficiently throughout the day.

01:27:27.375 --> 01:27:32.625
And believe it or not, the rate at
which fluid moves from your gut and

01:27:32.625 --> 01:27:36.584
into the cells and tissues of your body
and then from your bladder into urine

01:27:37.184 --> 01:27:41.594
is determined not just by the volume
of fluid you ingest, but also the rate

01:27:41.804 --> 01:27:45.105
at which you ingest that fluid and
you might be thinking, that's crazy.

01:27:45.405 --> 01:27:46.875
That makes no sense at all, right?

01:27:46.934 --> 01:27:49.665
If I drink a ton of fluid
slowly, doesn't it still mean

01:27:49.669 --> 01:27:51.105
that I'm going to urinate a ton?

01:27:51.554 --> 01:27:52.665
Yes and no.

01:27:52.785 --> 01:27:57.134
It also stands to reason that you
might ask, if I ingest very little

01:27:57.134 --> 01:28:00.735
fluid, but I do it very fast,
is it gonna be the case that I'm

01:28:00.735 --> 01:28:02.205
gonna urinate it out very quickly?

01:28:02.264 --> 01:28:03.464
Well, yes and no.

01:28:03.674 --> 01:28:08.205
The point is that the fluid filtration
systems of your body that range from

01:28:08.209 --> 01:28:12.915
the gut to the bladder and include the
kidney, of course, depend not just on the

01:28:12.915 --> 01:28:18.430
volume, but on the rate of fluid that you
ingest because of those Mechanosensors

01:28:18.450 --> 01:28:19.440
that we talked about earlier.

01:28:19.710 --> 01:28:23.849
If you gulp down a bunch of fluids,
you are going to excrete those fluids

01:28:23.849 --> 01:28:28.019
more quickly than if you sip them
slowly, excuse me, sip them slowly.

01:28:28.349 --> 01:28:30.269
So here's what I recommend
throughout the day.

01:28:30.275 --> 01:28:35.730
When you're trying to get your adequate
yield of water or other beverages,

01:28:35.790 --> 01:28:39.450
feel free to gulp that fluid or sip it.

01:28:40.530 --> 01:28:43.920
I'm a gulper, not a sipper, but
many of you are gonna be sippers,

01:28:43.925 --> 01:28:47.760
not gulpers, consume fluid at the
rate that feels right to you, but

01:28:47.764 --> 01:28:49.260
feel comfortable gulping that fluid.

01:28:49.260 --> 01:28:52.980
However, in the evening, if you are
somebody who has challenges with

01:28:53.130 --> 01:28:56.400
waking up excessively in the middle
of the night, reduce your fluid intake

01:28:56.400 --> 01:29:00.570
provided you hydrate properly throughout
the day, and I suggest consuming no

01:29:00.575 --> 01:29:06.300
more than 5, maybe 8 ounces of fluid
between the time of 10 hours after

01:29:06.305 --> 01:29:07.680
waking and when you go to sleep.

01:29:07.980 --> 01:29:11.160
Again, if you're very thirsty or you
under hydrate or it's very hot, feel

01:29:11.160 --> 01:29:13.680
free to ingest more fluid please.

01:29:14.099 --> 01:29:17.350
But most people will find that if they
reduce their fluid intake to about 5

01:29:17.370 --> 01:29:22.980
ounces or less of fluid in that later
part of the day, after 10 hours of having

01:29:22.980 --> 01:29:27.900
woken up and before sleep, and they sip
those beverages as opposed to gulping

01:29:27.900 --> 01:29:32.309
them, that they will have fewer bouts
of waking up in the middle of the night

01:29:32.309 --> 01:29:34.920
to go to the restroom and ideally zero.

01:29:35.430 --> 01:29:39.720
Let's talk about tap water, and here
I have to take a deep breath, not a

01:29:39.720 --> 01:29:45.030
deep gulp, but a deep breath because in
